## Block A: Power
- `+5V` source: USB through the Pico, or the off-board boost module on header `J_PWR`. Put a Schottky `D_pwr` [CHOOSE, 1N5819 or SS14 class] from the module's `+5V` into Pico VSYS (pin 39), so the module never back-feeds USB.
- Pico `3V3` (pin 36) → `FB1` → net `+3V3A`. `FB1` is one ferrite bead [CHOOSE, 0603, ~600 Ω at 100 MHz, ≥500 mA].
- `+3V3A` → `C_a1` (100 nF) → GND, and `+3V3A` → `C_a2` (10 µF) → GND.
- The OLED takes power from the plain Pico `3V3`, not from `+3V3A`, so its I2C noise stays off the analog rail.

## Block B: Bias boost (U3 = MAX5026)
- `+5V` → `FB2` (ferrite bead, second one from the BOM) → net `+5V_B`
- `+5V_B` → `C_in1` (10 µF) → GND, and `+5V_B` → `C_in2` (100 nF) → GND [CHOOSE]
- `+5V_B` → `L1` (47 µH) → switch node `LX`
- `LX` → `D2` (PMEG4010BEA, anode at `LX`) → net `VOUT_RAW`
- `VOUT_RAW` → `C_out` (≥50 V rating; value [DATASHEET]) → GND
- `VOUT_RAW` → `R_top` → `FB` pin → `RV1` (3296W 50 kΩ) and `R_bot` → GND
- `VOUT_RAW` → `BIAS` pin on `J_SIPM` and test pad `TP4`
- Pin-by-pin connections (EN/SHDN, COMP, any compensation parts): [DATASHEET]. These are in the MAX5026 typical application circuit.
- `R_top`, `R_bot`, `RV1` placement and values: [OGD]. The formula is `Vout = Vfb × (1 + R_top / (R_bot + RV1 section))`, with `Vfb` from the datasheet.
- Safety design: choose the divider so the pot's end stop cannot exceed 29.7 V. Then a mis-turned pot can't destroy the SiPM. I'll compute this when I have `Vfb` and the OGD values.

